# gui-boot-launch-lines.unaccounted: the `[richos] ...` lines src-tauri/src/main.rs can print that
# gui-boot.test.sh deliberately has NO rule for, one per line, each hole written `{}`.
#
# Why they are here: gui-boot's B2 fails on any line of a healthy boot that no rule accounts for.
# These are the failures, runtime events and not-running siblings of accounted lines that a healthy
# boot never prints. Leaving them without a rule is the point: if a healthy boot ever printed one,
# B2 would fail on it. gui-boot-launch-lines.py (test: gui-boot-launch-lines.test.sh) reads this list
# at every land that touches main.rs or gui-boot.test.sh:
#   - a new line in main.rs that no rule accounts for and that is not here fails it, so the author
#     decides: add a routine/resolved/gap rule to gui-boot.test.sh if a healthy boot prints it, or
#     append it here (exactly as the check prints it) if a healthy boot never does;
#   - a line here that main.rs no longer prints, or that a rule now accounts for, fails it too.
# Started from main.rs as it stood when the check was added; it is a classification nobody had
# written down, not a review of each line.
